===Etymology===
From {{prefix|en|un|earthly}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ʌnˈəːθ.li/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ʌnˈɝθ.li/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-unearthly.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-unearthly.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|unearthlier}}

# Not of the [[earth]]; non-[[terrestrial]].
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1898|author=H.G. Wells|authorlink=H.G. Wells|title={{w|The War of the Worlds}}|publisher=William Heinemann|location=London|page=205|passage=They were, I now saw, the most '''unearthly''' creatures it is possible to conceive.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2012|author=Charles Lockwood|title=Tragedy at Honda|page=65
|passage=In the hard glare of the Searchlight, which had been manned by Seaman 2nd class Evans W. Watkins, the rock had the '''unearthly''' look of a miniature satellite in space.}}
# [[preternatural|Preternatural]] or [[supernatural]].
#* {{RQ:Haggard She|passage=I believe that we are the last human beings who will ever see that '''unearthly''' sight.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=Steven Saylor|title=Roma: The Epic Novel of Ancient Rome|passage=As a fourth finger snapped, Cacus gave an '''unearthly''' scream and relented.}}
# [[strange|Strange]], [[enigmatic]], or [[mysterious]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1819|author=[publ. Sep 1858]|author2=James Morton|chapter=The Poetical Remains of the late Dr. John Leyden, with Memoirs of his Life|title=The Calcutta Review|volume=31|page=25
|passage=I then set out to survey the town in the self-same palankeen. The houses had all of them an '''unearthly''' appearance, by no means consonant to our ideas of Oriental splendor.}}
# [[ideal|Ideal]] beyond the [[mundane]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2000|author=Aileen Ribeiro|title=The Gallery of Fashion|page=42
|passage=By the late sixteenth century Elizabeth had become the icon-like Virgin Queen of legend, an image created, to a large extent, by her extraordinary, '''unearthly''' costume and appearance.}}
# [[ridiculous|Ridiculous]], [[ludicrous]], or [[outrageous]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1927|title=The Walther League Messenger|volume=36|page=225
|passage=I see my boys all wearing the same '''unearthly''' trousers, the same hair cuts, garish ties and sweaters, all rolling their socks and entertaining the same crazy notions about everything.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1961|month=May|author=Mark B. Warburton|title=Yatton and its branches to Clevedon and Wells|journal=Trains Illustrated|page=281|text=Two daily freight trips are run between Bristol West Depot and Wells. The first leaves Bristol at the '''unearthly''' hour of 3.50 a.m. and enters the branch at Yatton an hour later, over two hours before the first passenger train.}}

